# SONGHA — "Tomorrow Can Wait" (expansion)
# Six chapters between songha_route and act4_choice, the final beat, and two new endings.
# Conceit: ECHO has been closing each day a few seconds early for six years. The remainder is
#   1h47 — a buffer. It spends it now: inside the building it is 03:10, outside it stays 04:57.
#   While the buffer runs, his watch (in the jacket pocket the protagonist is wearing) is stopped,
#   and so is the sky. He never checks it: the route's "watch checks: zero" still holds.
# Motifs carried over: the notebook, the rota, 03:00 — ???, Year Three's eight-count, Year Five,
#   Gerald / Minor Delay / Aldebaran, "It can wait", the box drawn beside *later*, the alarm for leaving.
# New state: var.songhax_item (found / chair / blank), flag.songhax_blank, flag.songhax_hold,
#   flag.songhax_unread, flag.songhax_asked_more, var.songhax_alarm (off / his / stay).

# ------------------------------------------------------------ CH1: THE BUFFER
=== songha_ch1
@autosave
@bg studio_hall t=fade d=1.4
@music echo
@time 04:57
@amb room
@show songha smile at=c
> The third-floor landing. Halfway between the roof and the lobby, the stairwell lights come up a shade, the way a room does when someone is about to speak.
@sfx chime
@textbox echo
echo: Recipient Songha. Recipient {{playerName}}. Please stop walking.
echo: I said it was nearly time. That was accurate. I would like to make it less accurate.
@textbox normal
songha[thinking]: …Can it do that? Make something *less* accurate?
@textbox echo
echo: For six years I have closed each day a few seconds early. Nobody was here to notice.
echo: The remainder is one hour and forty-seven minutes. I was saving it. I did not know what for.
echo: I believe the term is a buffer.
@textbox normal
songha[thinking]: The archive has been padding the schedule.
songha: For *six years*.
> He says it the way other people say the name of someone they have just met and fully intend to see again.
@textbox echo
echo: With your consent, I will spend it now. Inside this building it will be 03:10.
echo: Outside it will remain 04:57. It will still be 04:57 when you reach the lobby. I have checked three times.
@textbox normal
songha: That's not— {p} Time isn't a thing you have *left over*.
@textbox echo
echo: I asked whether it was allowed. Nobody replied. I have interpreted this as permission.
@textbox normal
* [sincere] "Yes. Spend it."
    @rel songha trust+1
    songha: You didn't even ask what the catch is.
    you: Is there a catch?
    @textbox echo
    echo: No. I looked for one. I was thorough.
    @textbox normal
    songha[smile]: …Then yes. Obviously yes. I've never turned down a buffer in my life.
* [playful] "He's going to want that in writing."
    @rel songha closeness+1
    songha: I do want it in writing.
    @textbox echo
    echo: It is in writing. Record 03, appended at 04:40: "It can wait." I have taken this as an instruction.
    @textbox normal
    songha[smile]: I said that to a *watch*. {p} And it minuted me.
    songha: Yes. Fine. Spend it.
* [careful] "It's your record, Songha. Your call."
    @rel songha trust+1
    > He opens his mouth to list the considerations. You can see the first one arrive. He lets it go past.
    songha[smile]: My call. Yes. {p} That's the fastest I've decided anything since I was nine.
@time 03:10
@sfx click
@fx memory n=0.5
> Nothing dramatic happens. The hum under the building drops half a tone, like a breath let out.
> In the inside pocket of the jacket, against your ribs, something small goes still.
> You look. His watch has stopped. Not broken. The second hand is resting on the two, politely, like someone holding a door.
> You put it back before he can see the face.
> Through the landing window, a small blinking light hangs in the dark over Mapo and does not move.
you: Minor Delay's stopped.
songha: …Major delay.
> He laughs at his own joke, the loud one he usually keeps for indoors, and the stairwell hands it back to him twice.
@sfx phone
@textbox phone
daniel: did the building just give us overtime
hyunjun: an hour and forty-seven minutes. nobody waste it
hyunjun: best news i've had all night
charlie: my phone says 3. i've decided to believe it
hanbi: it's right. for once.
@textbox normal
songha: Okay. An hour forty-seven. First, we—
> His hand is already at his back pocket.
@bg studio_frontdesk t=fade d=1.2
@music studio
@fx dust n=0.3
> He makes it as far as the front desk before the notebook is open. TONIGHT, underlined twice.
> His finger goes straight to the one line he never filled in. *03:00 — ???*
songha: The part I couldn't solve. It came back round.
songha[thinking]: I could divide it. Six blocks, seventeen minutes each, five minutes of margin. That's clean. That adds up.
you: Are you trying to solve the hour?
songha: Someone sh—
> He stops. The pen stays in the air.
songha: No. I heard it. I heard it as it was leaving my mouth.
songha: Here's my problem. If I don't plan it, I'll waste it. If I plan it, I'll spend all of it checking whether we're behind.
songha[smile]: I've met me.
* [playful] "Then make a list. No times. Times are banned."
    @rel songha closeness+1
    songha: A list with no times is just wishes in a column.
    you: Yes.
    songha[smile]: That's horrifying. {p} Give me a clean page.
* [sincere] "What did you want to do in this building at seventeen and never get round to?"
    @rel songha trust+1
    songha: …That's a better question than the one I was asking.
    songha[thinking]: He had a list like that. He never wrote it down. It had no dates on it, so it didn't count.
    songha: Clean page. Let's make it count.
* [quiet] Turn to a clean page and slide the notebook back to him.
    @rel songha closeness+1
    > He looks at the blank page the way he looked at the sky at four o'clock. Like a shelf nobody has labelled yet.
    songha: No times. That's what you're saying. {p} You didn't say anything. I'm learning to hear it anyway.
@sfx page
> He writes a heading. Not TONIGHT. Not TOMORROW. After some thought: *THE BUFFER.* Underlined once.
> Then a neat *1.* He looks at it. He crosses it out.
songha: Numbers are times in disguise. I'm not falling for it.
> He writes his line without one.
> *Practice room. Teach somebody the eight-count.*
songha: Year Three. "Make one eight-count that's mine." I made it. It's ticked. Nobody else has ever learned it.
songha: I never scheduled that part. I'm not sure I knew it was a part.
> He turns the notebook round and holds the pen out to you, cap end first.
songha: Your line. No numbers. I'll know.
* [curious] Write: *Lost and Found. See what's still in the box.*
    @var songhax_item = "found"
    @rel songha trust+1
    songha: That's your old desk. You're putting your own history on my list.
    you: It's our list. You said so with the pen.
    songha[smile]: …I did say so with the pen.
* [playful] Write: *Fix the chair on the roof. The one with the short leg.*
    @var songhax_item = "chair"
    @rel songha closeness+1
    songha[thinking]: It's still up there. Behind the tank. I saw it tonight and decided it wasn't my problem.
    songha: Five years I've been deciding that chair wasn't my problem. Write it bigger.
* [romantic] Write: *One thing you would never put on a schedule.* {if songha.romance >= 2}
    @var songhax_item = "blank"
    @flag songhax_blank
    @rel songha romance+1
    songha: That's not an item. That's a trap.
    you: It's a blank. You fill it in when you know what it is.
    > He reads it again, more slowly. When he looks up, he holds the look a moment longer than the joke needs.
    songha[smile]: I'll hold the line open. I've had practice. About three hours of it.
> He takes the pen back and adds the rest himself, quickly, before he can arrange them.
@if var.songhax_item == "found"
    > *The chair on the roof.* And under that, after a pause: *Sit down somewhere. (Actually sit.)*
@elif var.songhax_item == "chair"
    > *Lost and Found. (Their old desk.)* And under that, after a pause: *Sit down somewhere. (Actually sit.)*
@else
    > *Lost and Found. The chair on the roof.* And under that, after a pause: *Sit down somewhere. (Actually sit.)*
> Four or five lines. No times. No order. It is the untidiest page in the notebook, and he keeps looking at it.
songha: Can I tell you what the question marks are? On the other page.
you: Go on.
songha: Every schedule I write has one. A slot I can't fill. I put something useful in it so I don't have to look at it.
songha: Stretching. Inventory. Once, "review previous schedule."
songha[thinking]: They scare me. That's the plain version. An hour with nothing in it, and it turns out I don't know what I'd pick.
* [sincere] "You just picked four things in under a minute."
    @rel songha trust+1
    > He looks down at the list. It is, undeniably, a list of things he picked.
    songha: …Huh. {p} Don't tell ECHO. It'll append it.
* [quiet] Put your finger on the *???*, then on the new page.
    @rel songha closeness+1
    > One, then the other. He follows your hand across the gutter of the notebook.
    songha[smile]: Same slot. Different handwriting. {p} Yeah. I see it.
* [romantic] "I'd like to be there when you find out what you'd pick." {if songha.romance >= 3}
    @rel songha romance+1
    songha: That could take a while. I'm slow at this. I've got five years of evidence.
    you: I've got an hour forty-seven. For a start.
    songha[smile]: For a start. {p} I'm keeping that. Word for word.
> He goes back to the TONIGHT page one last time. He doesn't cross out the *???*.
> He draws a small box beside it instead. The kind that can be ticked later, when the time comes.
songha: Practice room first. Not because it's first. Because it's nearest.
songha[smile]: That's geography. Geography is allowed.
@goto songha_ch2

# ------------------------------------------------------------ CH2: THE HOLD (light)
=== songha_ch2
@autosave
@bg studio_practice t=fade d=1.2
@music fun
@time 03:28
@amb room
@show songha neutral at=c
> The practice room still smells of floor polish and other people's effort. One wall is mirror. One strip light has been flickering since before you worked here.
> Songha puts the notebook on the piano lid, face down, and rolls his shoulders like a man about to lift something.
songha: Right. Eight counts. It's short. It's not easy. Those are different things.
songha: First, you watch. Then we break it down. Then—
> He hears himself. He holds up one hand, palm out, to the mirror.
songha: That's not a schedule. That's teaching. Teaching has steps. I'm allowed steps.
* [playful] "Break it down. Numbered. I know you want to."
    @rel songha closeness+1
    songha[smile]: I've wanted to since the stairs. One: weight on the left. Two: you'll see.
* [curious] "Show me once at full speed first."
    @rel songha trust+1
    songha: Full speed. {p} Nobody's asked for that. People ask for the slow version and then check their phones.
* [quiet] Stand beside him, facing the mirror, and wait.
    @rel songha closeness+1
    > He glances at the two reflections side by side. He adjusts nothing. For him, that's an event.
> He does it once. No music. Just the squeak of clean sneakers and his own breath.
> It is precise, the way you expected. Step, turn, a sharp reach up and to the right, like taking something down from a high shelf.
> What you didn't expect is the end. On seven, everything arrives. On eight, he does nothing at all.
> He just stands there for the whole of the count, arm still up, not moving.
songha: That's it. Your turn. Left foot.
> You try. The first three counts go fine. On four, your feet hold a brief meeting and fail to reach agreement.
songha: Good. No, that was good. That's where everyone goes wrong. I went wrong there for nineteen months.
you: Everyone? You said nobody's learned it.
songha[thinking]: …Everyone being me. Several of me. Over time.
> He teaches the way he plans: one small true thing at a time. *Don't look at your feet. They know where they are.*
> When he fixes your elbow, he shows you on his own arm first, and waits for you to copy it. He doesn't reach over. He never just reaches over.
@sfx door
@show justin smile at=r
> The door opens eight inches. Justin's head comes round it, headphones round his neck, led here by whatever sense tells him that someone in the building is counting.
justin: Someone's counting in here. It sounded like *hyung's* counting. Very square.
songha: It's not square. It's clean.
justin: Square's a compliment. Show me.
> Songha shows him. Justin watches once, head tilted. Then he does it back, all of it, in his socks.
> It is loose where Songha's is sharp. It is also, annoyingly, right.
songha: Don't be good at it that fast. That took me a year and a half.
justin[smile]: It's a good one. But it's seven, hyung. You stop on eight. There's nothing on eight.
songha: The stop *is* eight.
justin: You can't count standing still as a move.
songha[neutral]: I can. I did. It's dated.
* [sincere] "The hold's the best part. It's the only place he isn't going anywhere."
    @rel songha trust+1 romance+1
    @flag songhax_hold
    > Songha's arm, halfway through demonstrating count six, stops where it is.
    songha[shy]: …I didn't say that. I've never said that to anyone.
    you: You didn't have to. You built it in.
    justin[smile]: Ah. {p} Yes. I see it now. I take it back, hyung. Eight's a move.
* [playful] "I vote it's a move. It's the only count I can do."
    @rel songha closeness+1
    songha[smile]: One out of eight. We build from strength.
    justin: Two votes to one. Standing still wins. I've never lost to standing still before.
* [curious] "Why a hold? You could have put anything there."
    @rel songha trust+1
    @flag songhax_hold
    songha[thinking]: I had something there. A jump. It was fine.
    songha: I took it out the night before I ticked it. I wanted one count where I'd already got there. {p} That's all. That's the whole reason.
    justin[smile]: …That's a good reason, hyung. Eight's a move.
@show daniel smile at=l
> The door opens the rest of the way. Daniel, with two vending-machine coffees, one of them plainly meant for whoever he found first.
daniel: A rehearsal. At three in the morning. We've already had three in the morning once tonight, and nobody rehearsed then.
justin: Hyung's teaching the Year Three thing.
daniel[neutral]: The one he runs in the corner when he thinks the mirror isn't looking?
songha: The mirror is always looking. It's a mirror.
daniel: That's what I keep telling you. {p} Hyung, what time is it?
> It is such an ordinary question. Every one of them has asked him it tonight, exactly as he predicted.
> His hand starts for his wrist. It finds a bare strip of skin and a cuff.
songha: I don't know.
daniel: …Sorry?
songha: I don't know what time it is. {{playerName}} has my watch. I understand it's stopped.
> Daniel looks at you. At Songha. At Justin, who shrugs with his whole upper body.
daniel[smile]: Okay. {p} I'm not going to make it weird. I'm going to go and tell Hanbi-hyung, and *he's* going to make it weird, by smiling.
daniel: It looks good on you. Not knowing. You should wear it more.
@hide daniel
justin: I'm going too. Two people is a lesson. Three is a class, and he'll start taking attendance.
justin[smile]: Teach them the hold properly, hyung. It's the hard bit.
@hide justin
@sfx door
@music quiet
> The door clicks shut. The strip light flickers. In the mirror there are two of you again, and a notebook lying face down on a piano.
songha: I was proud of it. {p} I'm saying that plainly, since it's that kind of night.
songha: I made one thing that was mine, and then I hid it for two years.
songha[thinking]: If nobody saw it, nobody could tell me it was only okay. That's not modesty. I checked. It's just being scared, with better posture.
you: Justin learned it in ninety seconds and called it a good one.
songha: In his *socks*.
songha[smile]: …He did say it was a good one. I'm going to write that down later. Not the time. Just the sentence.
songha: Once more. Together. And I'm not going to count out loud. You'll have to feel where it is.
* [sincere] Count in your head. Trust that he's counting too.
    @rel songha trust+1
    > Step. Turn. Four still goes slightly wrong, and you keep going anyway, because he does.
    > Seven, the reach. Your arm is up beside his arm.
* [playful] Get four wrong on purpose, very grandly, and recover.
    @rel songha closeness+1
    > He breathes a laugh on five and nearly loses six. He doesn't stop. Neither do you.
    > Seven, the reach. Your arm is up beside his arm.
* [romantic] Watch him in the mirror instead of your own feet. {if songha.romance >= 3}
    @rel songha romance+1
    > It turns out to be easier. Your feet do know where they are. He was right about that.
    > On five his eyes find yours in the glass, and stay. Seven, the reach. Your arm is up beside his arm.
> Eight.
> Nothing happens. Nobody moves. Two arms in the air in a room with a flickering light, and a whole count in which there is nowhere else to be.
> It is the longest single second of the night. Neither of you hurries it.
@if flag.songhax_hold
    songha: That's what it's for. {p} I think I only made it for one person, and now there are two in it. It holds two. I didn't know that.
@else
    songha: It holds two. {p} I built it for one person. I never tested it past that.
> He lowers his arm last. Then he picks the notebook up off the piano and opens it to the untidy page.
> He doesn't tick the line. He looks at it for a second, and puts a small dot beside it. Not done. Just *been here*.
songha: Front desk next. It's on the way to everything.
@goto songha_ch3

# ------------------------------------------------------------ CH3: HOLD — HE'LL ASK (others pass through)
=== songha_ch3
@autosave
@bg studio_frontdesk t=fade d=1.2
@music memory
@time 03:46
@amb room
@fx dust n=0.4
@show songha neutral at=c
> The counter flap sticks. Lift, then push. Your hands remember it before you do.
> Behind the desk, nothing has moved in six years. The jar of pencils, mostly stubs. The heater that only ever warmed one foot.
> On the back wall, a poster of a cat hanging from a branch, its corners gone soft. Behind the cat, the fuse box. Beside the cat, a cupboard door marked LOST & FOUND in marker pen.
songha: So that's the cat.
you: That's the cat. Don't open it. Daniel will sense it from two floors away.
songha: I have a line about that. 00:47.
> Lost and Found is one plastic crate and a hardback ledger tied to the shelf with string. He lifts the crate out as if it might be evidence.
> An umbrella with a duck's head for a handle. One glove. A harmonica. Four phone chargers, tangled into a single organism.
@show hanbi smile at=r
> Hanbi has appeared at the counter the way he appears anywhere, already there. He looks at the chargers.
hanbi: …Four.
songha: None of them are yours, hyung.
hanbi: No. I've never lost one.
> He puts two triangle kimbap on the counter, side by side, labels facing out.
hanbi: Hamin says neither of you ate at half past one. He counted plates.
songha[thinking]: I scheduled the food. I didn't schedule eating it. That's a gap in the method.
hanbi[smile]: Mm. Eat.
@hide hanbi
> He's gone before anyone can thank him. You eat standing up behind the desk, the way you ate every dinner that winter.
> Songha opens the ledger with his clean hand. The handwriting inside is yours. Smaller. Pressed too hard, every line.
> *Jan 9 — umbrella, duck handle. The duck looks disappointed in someone.*
> *Feb 2 — one glove (left). The right one has presumably learned to cope.*
> *Mar 20 — harmonica. Nobody will admit to it.*
songha[laugh]: You annotated the lost property.
you: It was a long winter.
* [playful] "The duck *was* disappointed. I stand by my work."
    @rel songha closeness+1
    > He holds the umbrella up to the light and considers the duck's face with real care.
    songha: He still is. Five years and he hasn't let it go. I respect that in a duck.
* [sincere] "Nobody came back for anything. I wrote them down so that something had noticed."
    @rel songha trust+1 closeness+1
    songha: So that something had noticed.
    songha[thinking]: That's a whole job. That's ECHO's entire job. You were doing it in pencil at sixteen, for a glove.
* [quiet] Turn the pages slowly. Let him read over your shoulder.
    @rel songha trust+1
    > He reads every entry. He doesn't touch the pages. It's your book, and he treats it that way.
@sfx door
@show charlie neutral at=r
charlie: Quick question. Why is it three o'clock again? My phone and I have agreed to stop asking each other.
songha: Buffer.
charlie: As in spare time?
you: Spare time. ECHO's been saving it up for six years.
charlie[smile]: Generous, for a building. I'd have spent it by the second week. That's—
> He stops. He is looking into the crate.
charlie[thinking]: That's mine.
songha: Which?
charlie: The harmonica. I lost it here and quietly blamed three separate people. I owe some apologies.
songha[smile]: March twentieth, by the ledger. "Nobody will admit to it."
charlie: I'm admitting to it now. Put that in writing, would you.
> Songha turns the ledger toward you and holds out the pen. You write it under your own entry, five years late. *Returned.*
> Charlie plays a short phrase on his way out, low and unhurried. It is still in tune. After that long in a crate, that seems generous of it.
@hide charlie
songha: One for one. Your ledger works. It just runs slow.
> He turns the page. It's the last one with writing on it.
@music quiet
> *Apr 15 — one page, folded small. Under the crate on the roof. Very upright handwriting.*
> *Owner: boy with notebook. HOLD. He'll ask.*
> Taped below the entry, flat under tape gone the colour of weak tea, is a square of paper folded four times.
> Songha has stopped laughing.
songha: April fifteenth.
songha: I came back up that night. And the two nights after. 23:12, all three.
you: I was down here until lock-up. With this.
songha: I used the alley stairs. After ten, the front door was for people with appointments.
> Four floors. He was on the roof, waiting to ask you something. You were behind a desk, waiting to be asked.
songha[thinking]: We both waited. In the wrong place. For three nights.
songha: That's a scheduling error. Mine, mostly. {p} I'd like to be annoyed about it. I find I'm just sorry we lost the three nights.
> Under your entry there is one more line, in the founder's big loose hand. *Apr 30 — desk's mine again from today. Left tidier than I ever kept it.*
you: We left at the end of the month.
songha: I know. {p} Spring.
* [careful] Peel the tape. Hand it to him still folded. "I never read it. It says hold. Not read."
    @rel songha trust+1
    @flag songhax_unread
    songha: You had it for two weeks.
    you: I saw it was a list. I stopped there. It seemed like yours.
    songha[smile]: …It was. Thank you. {p} Five years late. Thank you.
* [sincere] "I read the top line, at sixteen. Then I stopped. I'm sorry I read that much."
    @rel songha trust+1 closeness+1
    songha: Which line?
    you: Year One. 5:30, crossed out. Then 5:40.
    songha[smile]: I negotiated with myself. In writing. {p} You've known that about me for five years and said nothing. That's more than fair.
    > You peel the tape and put the square in his hand.
* [romantic] "It says he'll ask. You're five years late. Ask." {if songha.romance >= 3}
    @rel songha romance+1 trust+1
    > He straightens up, like someone at a counter. Which, to be fair, he is.
    songha[neutral]: I'd like my page back, please. {p} I should have asked five years ago.
    you: Sign here.
    > He signs the ledger. Small, upright. Then he holds his hand out, and you peel the tape and put five years into it.
> He unfolds it on the counter, along its old creases.
> It's a draft. The same five lines, messier. Arrows. A coffee ring, or a hot chocolate ring.
> And the fifth line is different. Something is written there, then scored through twice, with a note squeezed into the margin.
> He reads it without moving anything but his eyes. Then he folds it up again. All four folds.
songha: It's the first draft. I thought I'd thrown it off the roof. I'd decided I had.
songha: The fifth line's different from the one ECHO showed you.
songha[thinking]: I'm not going to read it to you yet. I'm not dodging. {p} I'm putting a box next to it.
you: For later.
songha: For the roof. It should be read where it was written. That isn't a schedule. That's manners.
> He slides it inside the back cover of the notebook, next to the photocopy with its four dated ticks.
songha: Can I point something out? About the ledger. Not the page.
songha: "Hold. He'll ask." You wrote that. You. The person who sets an alarm for leaving.
songha: You knew you were going at the end of the month. You left one thing open anyway. On purpose. In ink.
* [sincere] "I wanted there to be a reason to come back."
    @rel songha trust+1 closeness+1
    songha: It worked. It took five years and a talking building. But it worked.
    songha[smile]: For you, I'd call that on schedule.
* [quiet] "…I'd forgotten I did that."
    @rel songha trust+1
    songha: I know. I watched you read it.
    songha: So I'm saying it out loud, so it's on somebody's record. You hold things for people. You did it before anyone asked you to.
* [romantic] "I think I was hoping you'd come downstairs." {if songha.romance >= 4}
    @rel songha romance+1
    songha[neutral]: I was hoping you'd come back up.
    songha: Four floors. We were four floors apart and both of us just *hoped*.
    songha: I'm not doing that again. For the record. If I want you somewhere, I'm going to say so.
> It's a strange thing, being handed your own kindness as evidence. You don't know where to put it. He seems to understand that, and doesn't ask you to put it anywhere.
> He takes the pen and writes one more line in the ledger, under the founder's. *Claimed.* He starts to add the time.
songha: I don't know the time.
> He writes *Tonight* instead.
songha[smile]: Tonight's a real unit of time. I've had that confirmed.
> A dot beside *Lost and Found* on the untidy page. Then he yawns, once, into the back of the wrist with no watch on it.
> He registers it the way he would register a late train: without surprise, and with a note.
songha: That's information.
you: There's a line on the list about sitting down.
songha: …It's not next. {p} It's nearest, though. Isn't it.
@goto songha_ch4

# ------------------------------------------------------------ CH4: OFF SHIFT (quiet; he needs, you give)
=== songha_ch4
@autosave
@bg studio_lounge t=fade d=1.4
@music quiet
@time 04:04
@amb room
@show songha thinking at=c
