> Above his own name, in the space that used to be the heading, he has written yours. And after it, the bracket that was always his. *(list)*.
@show daniel laugh at=r
daniel: He saved *two* to-do lists and a staff rota. Nobody has ever been more himself.
songha: One of them is a draft.
@show hyunjun thinking at=c
hyunjun: There's a name above hyung's. In six years there has never been a name above hyung's.
@hide hyunjun
@show hanbi smile at=c
hanbi: It means he's got somebody to hand it to.
hanbi: …Good. It was heavy. I could see it from the sofa.
@hide daniel hanbi
@show hamin smile at=r
hamin: Then you both have to eat properly. That's a two-person job now. I'll be counting plates.
@hide hamin
@show justin smile at=r
justin: Two people. That's a lesson, not a class. No attendance, hyung.
songha[laugh]: I'm taking attendance. There are two of us. It'll be quick.
@hide justin
@show charlie smile at=r
charlie: So what are you two now? Is there a word for it?
> Songha thinks about it. Properly, the way he thinks about a count that isn't sitting right.
songha[smile]: There's a word. I'm going to say it upstairs. It needs a better ceiling than this.
@hide charlie
@call echo_farewell
@bg dawn_rooftop t=slow d=3
@amb birds
@music dawn
@time 06:14
@fx light n=0.8
> At 05:40 he walked everyone to the station, exactly as written. He counted seven heads through the barrier and said "text when you're home" seven times.
> Then he turned round instead of going through. You had already turned round.
> The foreman with the hard hat gave you the roof until half past six, in exchange for nothing. People are kind at that hour.
> So it's the chair that doesn't wobble and the crate, and two warm cans from the shop by the station, and a sky going the colour of peach skin at one edge.
@show songha smile at=c
songha: 06:14. Sunrise. It's on time. I take no credit.
> He has the notebook open to TOMORROW. The line that's been empty since four o'clock. *07:00 —*
songha: I know what goes there now.
> He writes it and turns the page toward you, a quarter-inch, the way he did at midnight. *07:00 — breakfast. (You pick.)*
songha: Okay. The word. I said I'd say it up here.
songha: I've got seven people I'd do anything for. I know their shoe sizes. I know who's lying about having slept.
songha[thinking]: What I've never had is someone I hand the list to. I didn't know it was missing. You don't, until somebody holds it for ten minutes and nothing falls down.
songha: You're my person. That's the word. I looked for a better one on the stairs. There isn't one.
you: You're mine. I've never stayed anywhere long enough to have one. I'd like to start with you.
> He nods, once, like a thing confirmed. Then the nod gives up and turns into the whole smile.
songha: It's not a smaller thing. I want that said plainly. It's the thing I didn't have.
songha: So. When you were sixteen, you told me you didn't know what you were doing on Thursday.
you: I remember.
songha: You do now. Thursdays. You and me. Anywhere with a table.
songha[smile]: It's not a plan. It's a standing item. Standing items don't get ticked. They just keep happening.
you: What happens on a Thursday?
songha: You tell me what went wrong that week. I give you three things I'd do about it. You ignore two.
songha: Then you take the notebook for an hour, and I sit down. Actually sit.
you: And when it's 5:40 and it's heavy?
songha: Then I text you. That's the rule. I've never had that rule. {p} I like it already.
songha: And when you feel like setting the alarm—
you: I text you.
songha: Before. Not after, from another city. Before. I'll answer. I'm up at 5:40. I'm famously available.
> You laugh. He laughs, the loud one, and doesn't cover his mouth. A pigeon leaves the water tank in protest.
> The sun clears the rooftops. Below, the renovation crew are carrying the first things out of Studio 25: a sofa cushion, a lamp, the rubber plant.
you: My letter asked whether I'd found a place I want to stay.
songha: And?
you: I don't think it's a place. I think it's a Thursday.
songha[smile]: I'm going to write that down. Not the time. Just the sentence.
> You take out your phone. You open the clock, and you do something you have never done in any city. You set an alarm for arriving.
> *Thursday — go back.*
> He reads it upside down. He reads everything upside down. He doesn't say a word about it, which from him is an entire speech.
> His watch is still ticking in the pocket of the jacket you're still wearing. He hasn't asked for either.
you: Hold. He'll ask.
songha[smile]: I'll ask. Thursday.
> The building behind you is emptying. The morning isn't. You stay on the good chair and the crate until the cans are finished.
> Two names at the top of a rota, and nothing at all written under *07:00* but breakfast.
@ending songha_beside
@credits
@end

# ------------------------------------------------------------ ENDING: SOMEDAY — "When Gerald Returns"
=== songha_ending_someday
@bg studio_lobby t=fade d=1.6
@music echo
@time 04:58
@cam wide
@show songha thinking at=l
> He takes that in. You watch him not ask what it is. You watch him want to, very much, and set it down.
songha: Then don't say it in forty seconds.
songha[smile]: I'd rather have it properly. I can wait. I've recently learned how. There's a record of it.
> On the Wall, every box goes dark but one.
@textbox echo
echo: Record 03 selected. Owner: Songha. Writing to glass.
echo: Contents: one page. Five items. Year Five: open.
echo: Appended: one first draft. One word amended by the owner tonight, from "replace" to "keep".
echo: Appended: one item, not yet said. I have left room for it on the glass. I am learning to leave room.
@textbox normal
> The card slides out, warm and faintly lit. In one corner there is a small clear space with nothing engraved in it. It catches the light differently from the rest.
@show daniel laugh at=r
daniel: There's a blank bit. ECHO left him a blank bit. Hyung, you've infected the building.
songha: It's not blank. It's reserved.
@show hyunjun smile at=c
hyunjun: Reserved for what?
songha[neutral]: Go away, Hyunjun.
hyunjun[smile]: Understood. {p} Still not a no, though.
@hide daniel hyunjun
@show justin smile at=r
justin: It's the hold, hyung. You're on eight. Don't rush it.
@hide justin
@show haruta smile at=r
haruta: A scene that ends just before the line. I like those. The line plays over the next scene.
@hide haruta
@show hanbi smile at=r
> Hanbi says nothing. He looks at the clear corner of the glass, and then at the two of you, and he puts his pen back in his coat without ever having taken the cap off.
@hide hanbi
> Songha holds the card out for you to carry. His thumb covers the blank corner until the last moment, as though it could get cold.
@call echo_farewell
@bg dawn_rooftop t=slow d=3
@amb birds
@music songha
@time 05:40
@fx light n=0.6
> The stairwell was dark, so he went first with the phone torch and said "step" at every step. There were forty-one. He didn't count them aloud. You heard him counting anyway.
> The roof at 05:40 is grey and soft, and smells of rain drying. The good chair. The crate. One last star, low in the south.
@show songha neutral at=c
> He stands with his hands in his hoodie pocket and doesn't prompt you. It may be the hardest thing he has done tonight, and tonight he tore a page out of his own notebook.
you: Properly, then.
you: I like you. Not the list, and not the jacket. You. The person who asks first, every time.
> He doesn't answer at once. He doesn't reach for the pen either.
songha: Give me a second. Not to plan. Just to have heard it.
> You give him the second. He takes three.
songha[shy]: I like you too. {p} There. No steps.
songha: I liked you at seventeen for calling a star Gerald. I liked you again from about half past midnight. Those are two separate likings, and I've got both of them.
songha[smile]: Okay. So. First, we—
> He stops. He shuts his eyes for a moment, and laughs under his breath.
songha: I was about to schedule it. I had a first step ready. It was a good first step.
you: What was it?
songha: I'm not telling you. It'll keep.
> He sits down on the crate. You take the chair. The sky does the slow thing skies do.
songha: Here's the honest part. This is the year on the page. The next few months are going to take everything I've got, and I want to give them that.
songha[thinking]: I don't want to give you whatever's left over at eleven at night. I've seen what's left over. It's a man reading a rota.
you: Here's mine. I've only just stopped setting alarms for leaving. About an hour ago.
you: I'd like to learn to stay somewhere before I ask someone to be the reason.
> He listens to the whole of it. He doesn't offer a single suggestion. You can see him not doing it.
songha: So it's not a no.
you: It's the opposite of a no. It's "hold. He'll ask."
songha[laugh]: You're quoting the ledger at me.
you: It's a good ledger. It works. It just runs slow.
songha: Then it arrives when it arrives. Both of us decide that. Not me on my own with a pen. {p} I want that on the record.
you: ECHO's off. There's no record.
songha[smile]: Then you'll have to remember it the hard way. So will I.
> He looks east, low down, where the rooftops meet the sky. There is nothing there yet.
songha: ECHO said Gerald comes back every winter. Before dawn. Nobody schedules it.
songha: The first clear morning he's back. That's when.
songha: I'm going to try not to look up the date. I give myself a week.
you: Where?
songha: Haneul Park. The top of the stairs. It faces east, and there's nothing in the way.
songha[thinking]: Two hundred and ninety-one steps. I've counted. Obviously I've counted.
you: What time?
songha: 5:40. I'll be awake anyway. That morning it gets to be for something else.
you: What do I bring?
songha: The watch. Keep it till then. You can give it back up there. Or not. We'll know.
songha: I'll bring the draft, and the pen. And two cans. I know which.
you: And after that?
songha[smile]: I'm not writing the line after that. I'm leaving it open on purpose. Second time in my life. It's getting easier.
> He holds out his hand. Not to shake on it. Palm up, open, the way you would offer to take something heavy from someone.
songha: I don't shake on things without dates. This is just my hand. {p} If you'd like it.
> You take it. It's warm. He holds on for a count of eight, and you both know where the hold is.
> The last star goes out in the south. The east stays empty, a clean strip of sky above the laundromat, with plenty of room in it.
> Somewhere below that line, on nobody's schedule, a faint orange star is already on its way.
> You know its real name now. You'll call it Gerald when it gets here. He'll let you.
@ending songha_someday
@credits
@end
